  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Skies remain mostly sunny across\nnorthern and central Nevada this afternoon. Winds are light across\nthe forecast area with temperatures in the 50s. Look for a few\nhigh clouds moving into the area during the overnight with lows in\nthe 20s and 30s.\n\nThe west-southwest flow over the forecast area will continue for\ntomorrow. An upper level low pressure system will shift southward\nand over the Pacific Northwest states. Winds are projected to be\non the breezy side across central Nevada with south to southwest\nwinds 10 to 20 mph with gusts up to 30 mph. A weak impulse in the\nflow is expected to move through central and then northeastern\nNevada Thursday afternoon with a few light showers over the\nmountains in northeastern Nevada. Highs will be in the 40s and\n50s. Light showers are expected to develop during the early\nmorning hours Friday with plenty of clouds and lows in the 30s\nand 40s.\n\nFor Friday through the upcoming weekend, models continue to\nportray a rather complex upper level trough setup. This will start\nout with the aforementioned large parent longwave trough digging \nsouth along the wast coast. Through the period, models are\ncontinuing to suggest several distinct areas of circulation, from\nas far north as British Columbia, Canada south to Baja California.\nThese features will act as a focal point for the development of \nshowers and thunderstorms, especially during the latter portions\nof the weekend for northern and central Nevada. For Friday \nthrough Sunday. These disturbances will bring several rounds of \nisolated to scattered showers, isolated thunderstorms, and \nmountain snowfall through Monday morning. With this system \nstill well offshore, models will continue to be highly variable \nform run to run. Current multi-day precipitation amounts look to \nbe about a tenth to a half-inch of rain for valley locations, \nwith a half-inch to an inch of water for the higher passes and \nmountains. Snow levels will again start high at 9000 feet, \ndropping to about 7000 feet by Sunday. Snow amounts are coming in\nwith a trace to a half-inch possible for the higher elevation \npasses and summits, and six to twelve inches of snow for the \nhighest elevation peaks. To be fair, there is still a lot of \nunknowns with this system, the first being is how are all of the \nvorticity centers portrayed in model solutions will interact with \neach other. The second unknown will be the strength of these \ncenters.\n\nFor the early portions of next week, beginning Monday and Tuesday\nhigh pressure will build in as the trough departs bringing \nquieter weather back into the region. Temperatures will be near \nto, to slightly below normal for the nest week, as high will range\nin the upper 40s to upper 50s, with lows in the mid 20s to upper \n30s. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: No changes were made to the\nshort term NBM grids. Confidence remains high in quiet across the\narea with breezy conditions over central Nevada for Thursday\nafternoon. South to southwest winds of 10 to 15 mph with gusts up\nto 30 mph are possible. \n\nThere is high confidence for the potential for several rounds of \nisolated to scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms starting\nlate Thursday afternoon/evening, and persisting on and off \nthrough early Monday morning.\n\nThere is high confidence in the return of upper ridging next Monday\nafternoon through at least Tuesday of next week which will bring \nquiet and dry conditions to the region.",
